  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The usual prompt is “how do you prepare features for a model?” or the more pointed “why scale, and how do you
    handle categoricals?”. Here is a complete answer in about ninety seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “For numeric features I scale, because distance-based models and gradient descent are sensitive to units: an
      unscaled feature with large values dominates Euclidean distances and makes the loss surface ill-conditioned so
      gradient descent zigzags, and weight penalties act in a unit-dependent way. I standardize by
      default, use min-max when I need a bounded range and there are no outliers, robust scaling for heavy tails, and
      a log transform for positive skewed quantities. Tree-based models do not need scaling since splits are invariant
      to monotone transforms.
    </p>
    <p>
      For categorical features I distinguish nominal from ordinal. Nominal categories get one-hot encoded so each gets
      an independent effect; with an intercept I drop one column to avoid the dummy variable trap, and I set the
      encoder to handle unseen categories. Ordinal categories get integer codes in their natural order. For
      high-cardinality features I use out-of-fold target encoding with smoothing, hashing, or learned embeddings in a
      neural network, and I am careful about leakage with target encoding.
    </p>
    <p>
      All of these transformations learn statistics, so they are fitted on the training split only and applied
      unchanged to validation, test and production data; I wrap them with the model in a Pipeline so every split
      and deployment get this right automatically. Fitting the scaler on the full dataset before splitting is a
      leakage bug.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Does scaling change a linear regression’s predictions?” With the exact normal equation and no regularization, no; coefficients rescale but predictions are identical. With gradient descent (speed) or a penalty (which weights are shrunk), yes.</li>
    <li>“Why not label-encode cities for a random forest?” It imposes an arbitrary order; trees can partially undo it with multiple splits but it is still worse than one-hot or native categorical handling for most libraries.</li>
    <li>“What goes wrong with target encoding and how do you fix it?” The row’s own label leaks into its feature; compute out-of-fold and smooth towards the global mean.</li>
    <li>“A test row has a category never seen in training.” Encoder configured to ignore (all zeros) or map to an “other” bucket; for embeddings, a reserved unknown row.</li>
    <li>“What is an embedding, in these terms?” A learned, dense encoding of a categorical variable: one-hot followed by a weight matrix, trained end to end.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="Give the three reasons feature scaling matters, with one model affected by each.">
    <p>Distances (kNN, k-means, SVM, PCA): the largest-range feature dominates. Gradient descent (linear/logistic regression, neural nets): a long narrow valley, slow zigzag. Weight penalties (ridge, lasso, week 3): unit-dependent.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Which common models do not need feature scaling, and why?">
    <p>Decision trees, random forests, gradient boosting, Naive Bayes. Tree splits compare a feature with a threshold, which is unchanged by any monotone rescaling; Naive Bayes models each feature separately.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Standardization versus min-max scaling: definitions, output ranges, and how each reacts to an outlier.">
    <p>Standardization: <Math tex="(x-\mu)/\sigma" />, mean 0 and std 1, unbounded; an outlier becomes a large z-score and inflates <Math tex="\sigma" />. Min-max: <Math tex="(x-\min)/(\max-\min)" />, exactly [0, 1]; an outlier becomes 1 and crushes all other values towards 0.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="When would you use robust scaling or a log transform?">
    <p>Robust scaling (median and IQR) for heavy-tailed features where a few extremes would distort mean and std. Log transform for positive, right-skewed, multiplicative quantities such as income or counts, to make the distribution closer to symmetric before modelling.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why must a scaler be fitted on the training set only? Name two consequences of fitting it on all data.">
    <p>Its statistics are part of the model. Fitting on all data leaks test-set information (means, ranges, categories) into training, giving optimistic evaluation; and it does not match deployment, where new rows must be transformed with frozen training statistics.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the dummy variable trap?">
    <p>One-hot columns for all <Math tex="k" /> categories sum to the constant intercept column, so the design matrix is perfectly collinear and <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is singular. Drop one column; the dropped category is the reference.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why is integer-coding a nominal feature a mistake for a linear model? Is it a mistake for a tree?">
    <p>It invents an order and equal spacing: Tokyo = 2 is treated as twice London = 1. A tree only uses the order to choose split points and can isolate categories with several splits, so it is less harmful but still arbitrary and usually worse than one-hot or native categorical support.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How do you handle a category at inference time that was not present in training?">
    <p>Configure the encoder: all-zero one-hot (reference effect) via <code>handle_unknown="ignore"</code>, an explicit “other” bucket, hashing (handled by construction), or a reserved unknown row in an embedding table.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Describe target encoding and its main danger.">
    <p>Replace each category with the mean target among training rows of that category. Danger: leakage, since a row’s own label enters its feature, especially for rare categories. Fix: out-of-fold encoding and smoothing towards the global mean.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is an embedding, and how is it related to one-hot encoding?">
    <p>A learned dense vector per category stored in a lookup table. Mathematically it is a one-hot vector multiplied by the table, that is, one-hot encoding followed by a weight matrix, trained end to end.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Does standardizing features change the predictions of plain linear regression (ordinary least squares)? Of linear regression with the week 3 penalty on big weights (called ridge regression)?">
    <p>OLS by the normal equation: no, predictions are identical and coefficients rescale. Ridge: yes, because the L2 penalty depends on the weights’ scale, so different features are shrunk differently depending on their units.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How would you encode hour of day?">
    <p>As a cyclic feature: <Math tex="\sin(2\pi h/24)" /> and <Math tex="\cos(2\pi h/24)" />, so hour 23 is adjacent to hour 0. Plain integer coding puts them 23 apart; one-hot loses the adjacency entirely.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first, then check. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. Standardize the column (2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9). (Mean 5, population std 2.)">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(-1.5, -0.5, -0.5, -0.5, 0, 0, 1, 2)" />. Check: the values average to 0.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Min-max scale the same column, then say what happens to the scaled values if a new value 25 is added and the scaler is refitted.">
    <p>
      Range 2 to 9: <Math tex="(0, 0.286, 0.286, 0.286, 0.429, 0.429, 0.714, 1)" />. With 25 added the range is 2 to 23, so the original 9 drops to <Math tex="7/23 = 0.30" /> and everything is squashed below 0.31 while 25 becomes 1.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Robust-scale (20,000, 25,000, 30,000, 35,000, 1,000,000) using median 30,000, Q1 25,000, Q3 35,000. Compare with the z-score of the outlier (mean 222,000, std 389,000).">
    <p>
      <Math tex="(-1, -0.5, 0, 0.5, 97)" />. The four ordinary incomes keep a spread of 1 between quartiles, and the outlier is at 97, clearly flagged. The z-scores were <Math tex="(-0.52, -0.51, -0.49, -0.48, 2.0)" />: the ordinary incomes are indistinguishable and the outlier is only 2.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Customers A = (age 30, income 50,000), B = (31, 51,000), C = (60, 50,100). Compute Euclidean distances from A unscaled, then after dividing age by 10 and income by 10,000. Who is A’s nearest neighbour each time?">
    <p>
      Unscaled: <Math tex="d(A,B) = \sqrt{1 + 10^6} \approx 1000" />, <Math tex="d(A,C) = \sqrt{900 + 10^4} \approx 104" />: C. Scaled: <Math tex="d(A,B) = \sqrt{0.01 + 0.01} = 0.14" />, <Math tex="d(A,C) = \sqrt{9 + 0.0001} = 3.0" />: B.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. One-hot encode the column (red, blue, green, blue, red) with all columns, then with drop-first (alphabetical: blue dropped). Write both matrices.">
    <p>
      Columns (blue, green, red): rows (0,0,1), (1,0,0), (0,1,0), (1,0,0), (0,0,1). Drop blue → columns (green, red): (0,1), (0,0), (1,0), (0,0), (0,1). The all-zero rows are blue, the reference.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. A linear model with intercept uses the full one-hot of problem 5. Show that the three columns plus the intercept column are linearly dependent.">
    <p>
      blue + green + red = (1, 1, 1, 1, 1) = the intercept column. One column is an exact combination of the others, so <Math tex="X^\top X" /> is singular and coefficients are not identifiable (adding <Math tex="c" /> to every colour weight and subtracting <Math tex="c" /> from the intercept gives identical predictions).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. A dataset has 10 numeric features and 3 categoricals with 4, 12 and 3,000 categories. How many columns after one-hot encoding all three? What would you do instead for the third?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="10 + 4 + 12 + 3000 = 3026" /> columns, nearly all zeros. For the 3,000-category feature use out-of-fold target encoding, hashing into a few hundred buckets, or an embedding if using a neural model.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. Naive target encoding on a training set: category Z appears once, with target 500. What value does that row get, and why is that a problem? How does smoothing with the global mean 200 and weight m = 10 change it?">
    <p>
      The row gets 500, its own label: the feature equals the answer and the model will trust it blindly, then fail on new Z rows. Smoothed: <Math tex="(1 \cdot 500 + 10 \cdot 200)/(1 + 10) = 227" />, mostly the global mean because one observation is weak evidence. Out-of-fold computation would additionally exclude the row’s own label.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. Encode the days of the week for (a) a tree model and (b) a linear model that should know weekends are special and Sunday is next to Monday.">
    <p>
      (a) Integer codes 0–6 or native categorical support; trees will find the weekend split. (b) A binary is_weekend feature plus cyclic <Math tex="\sin(2\pi d/7), \cos(2\pi d/7)" />; or one-hot if you want a free effect per day (then Sunday–Monday adjacency is not encoded).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. You standardized the data, then split into train and test, then trained. The test RMSE is 10. In production the first row arrives alone. What happens, and what should the pipeline have been?">
    <p>
      There is no stored mean or std, so the row cannot be scaled consistently; scaling it by itself gives NaN or zeros. Also the reported 10 is slightly optimistic because test statistics leaked into the scaler. Correct pipeline: split first; fit the scaler on train inside a Pipeline with the model; save the pipeline; transform production rows with the frozen training statistics.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="11. Implement StandardScaler and MinMaxScaler yourself as classes with fit, transform and inverse_transform. Test against scikit-learn, including that inverse_transform(transform(X)) recovers X.">
    <NewInCode items={[["class MyStandardScaler: and def fit(self, X):", "A class bundles data and functions together. Each function inside it receives the object itself as self, so fit can store what it learned (self.mean_) and transform can use it later. This is exactly how scikit-learn’s scalers are built.", 'python/classes'], ["MinMaxScaler()", "Rescales each column so its smallest training value is 0 and its largest is 1.", "sklearn/minmax-scaler"], ["np.allclose(a, b)", "True if every pair of numbers is equal up to tiny rounding differences.", "numpy/isclose"], ["assert condition", "Stops with an error if the condition is false: a built-in self-check.", "python/assert"]]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler

class MyStandardScaler:
    def fit(self, X):
        # one average per column, learned from X
        self.mean_ = X.mean(axis=0)
        self.scale_ = X.std(axis=0)      # one spread per column
        # returning the object itself is the scikit-learn habit
        return self

    def transform(self, X):
        return (X - self.mean_) / self.scale_

    def inverse_transform(self, Z):
        # undo: multiply back, add back
        return Z * self.scale_ + self.mean_

class MyMinMaxScaler:
    def fit(self, X):
        self.min_ = X.min(axis=0)
        self.range_ = X.max(axis=0) - X.min(axis=0)
        return self

    def transform(self, X):
        return (X - self.min_) / self.range_

    def inverse_transform(self, Z):
        return Z * self.range_ + self.min_

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
X_train = rng.normal(50, 10, size=(100, 3))
X_test = rng.normal(55, 12, size=(20, 3))

pairs = [(MyStandardScaler(), StandardScaler()), (MyMinMaxScaler(), MinMaxScaler())]
for mine, theirs in pairs:
    # both learn from the training rows only
    mine.fit(X_train)
    theirs.fit(X_train)
    assert np.allclose(mine.transform(X_test), theirs.transform(X_test))
    round_trip = mine.inverse_transform(mine.transform(X_test))
    # scaling then unscaling gives back the original
    assert np.allclose(round_trip, X_test)
print("both scalers match scikit-learn")

# Output:
# both scalers match scikit-learn`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. Show the dummy variable trap numerically: build a design matrix with an intercept and a full one-hot, print the rank of XᵀX and say whether it can be inverted; then drop one column and repeat.">
    <NewInCode items={[["OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False, drop=\"first\")", "sparse_output=False returns a normal array you can print. drop=\"first\" leaves out the first category’s column.", "sklearn/one-hot"], ["np.ones(len(H))", "A column of ones: the intercept column.", "numpy/ones"], ["np.linalg.matrix_rank(G)", "How many truly independent columns a matrix has. Fewer than its number of columns means it cannot be inverted.", "numpy/linalg-rank"]]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder

# one column
cities = np.array([["London"], ["Paris"], ["Tokyo"], ["Paris"], ["London"], ["Tokyo"]])

# all three city columns
full_encoder = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False)
full = full_encoder.fit_transform(cities)
# drops the first city (London)
dropped_encoder = OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False, drop="first")
dropped = dropped_encoder.fit_transform(cities)

for name, H in [("full one-hot", full), ("drop first", dropped)]:
    # put the intercept column of ones in front
    X = np.column_stack([np.ones(len(H)), H])
    G = X.T @ X
    # how many truly independent columns there are
    rank = np.linalg.matrix_rank(G)
    print(name, "| columns:", X.shape[1], "| rank of X^T X:", rank)
    if rank < X.shape[1]:
        print("   singular: one column is a sum of the others, the dummy variable trap")
    else:
        print("   invertible: the normal equation has one answer")
# Why not just call np.linalg.inv and see if it fails? Rounding errors often
# let inv return
# a matrix of huge, meaningless numbers instead of raising an error. The
# rank is the honest test.

# Output:
# full one-hot | columns: 4 | rank of X^T X: 3
#    singular: one column is a sum of the others, the dummy variable trap
# drop first | columns: 3 | rank of X^T X: 3
#    invertible: the normal equation has one answer`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="13. Demonstrate target-encoding leakage: create a nominal feature with 500 random categories and a target that is pure noise. Compare the validation R² of a linear model using naive in-sample target encoding against scikit-learn’s TargetEncoder.">
    <NewInCode items={[["rng.integers(0, 500, n)", "n random whole numbers from 0 to 499, used here as category codes.", "numpy/rng-integers"], ["zip(cat_train, y_train)", "Walks through two arrays side by side, one pair at a time.", "python/zip-enumerate"], ["totals.get(c, 0.0)", "Looks up c in the dictionary, or gives 0.0 if c is not in it yet.", 'python/dict-get'], [".reshape(-1, 1)", "Turns a flat array into a single column. -1 means “as many rows as needed”.", "numpy/reshape"], ["LinearRegression().fit(X, y)", "Makes and trains a model in one line; fit hands back the trained model.", "sklearn/fit-predict"], ["TargetEncoder(target_type=\"continuous\")", "Replaces each category by a smoothed average of the answer for that category, computed so that a row never sees its own answer.", "sklearn/target-encoder"], ["r2_score(y_true, y_pred)", "How much of the error of always guessing the average the predictions remove: 1 is perfect, 0 is no better, below 0 is worse.", "sklearn/r2"]]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.preprocessing import TargetEncoder
from sklearn.metrics import r2_score

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
n = 2000
category = rng.integers(0, 500, n)       # 500 categories, about 4 rows each
# pure noise: the target has NOTHING to do with the category
y = rng.normal(size=n)
cat_train, cat_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    category, y, test_size=0.3, random_state=0)

# Naive encoding: each category's average target over the training rows,
# computed INCLUDING each row's own answer.
totals = {}
counts = {}
for c, target in zip(cat_train, y_train):
    # .get(c, 0.0): the total so far, or 0 if c is new
    totals[c] = totals.get(c, 0.0) + target
    counts[c] = counts.get(c, 0) + 1
overall_mean = y_train.mean()

def naive_encode(cats):
    values = []
    for c in cats:
        if c in counts:
            values.append(totals[c] / counts[c])
        else:
            # a category never seen in training
            values.append(overall_mean)
    # one column, as scikit-learn expects
    return np.array(values).reshape(-1, 1)

naive_train = naive_encode(cat_train)
naive_test = naive_encode(cat_test)
naive_model = LinearRegression().fit(naive_train, y_train)
naive_train_r2 = r2_score(y_train, naive_model.predict(naive_train))
naive_test_r2 = r2_score(y_test, naive_model.predict(naive_test))
print("naive   train R^2:", round(naive_train_r2, 2), "  test R^2:", round(naive_test_r2, 2))

# Proper: TargetEncoder encodes each training row without using that row's
# own answer, and smooths rare categories.
encoder = TargetEncoder(target_type="continuous", random_state=0)
proper_train = encoder.fit_transform(cat_train.reshape(-1, 1), y_train)
proper_test = encoder.transform(cat_test.reshape(-1, 1))
proper_model = LinearRegression().fit(proper_train, y_train)
proper_train_r2 = r2_score(y_train, proper_model.predict(proper_train))
proper_test_r2 = r2_score(y_test, proper_model.predict(proper_test))
print("proper  train R^2:", round(proper_train_r2, 2), "  test R^2:", round(proper_test_r2, 2))

# Output:
# naive   train R^2: 0.33   test R^2: -0.33
# proper  train R^2: 0.0   test R^2: -0.0
# naive: train R^2 clearly above 0 (it 'learned' noise from its own
# answers), test R^2 below 0.
# proper: both about 0, which is the truth: the category carries no
# information.`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Data where one feature drifts between the training period and the test period. Show that fitting the scaler on all the rows changes nothing for plain linear regression, but makes a nearest-neighbour classifier look better than it really is.">
    <NewInCode items={[["np.concatenate([a, b])", "Joins arrays end to end into one longer array.", "numpy/stack"], ["(stable > 0).astype(int)", "stable > 0 gives True or False for every row; astype(int) turns them into 1 and 0, the class labels.", "numpy/astype"], ["X[:n_train] and X[n_train:]", "The first n_train rows, and all the rows after them.", "numpy/slicing"]]} />
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np
from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler
from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline
from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression
from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
n_train = 160
n_test = 40
# Feature 1 is a sensor that drifts upward after the training period;
# feature 2 is stable.
drift = np.concatenate([rng.normal(10, 1, n_train), rng.normal(14, 1, n_test)])
stable = rng.normal(0, 1, n_train + n_test)
X = np.column_stack([drift, stable])
# the class depends only on the stable feature
label = (stable > 0).astype(int)
# a number to predict, for the linear model
price = 2 * drift + stable + rng.normal(0, 1, n_train + n_test)

# the first 160 rows: the past
X_train = X[:n_train]
# the last 40 rows: the future
X_test = X[n_train:]

# (a) Leaky: the scaler learns from ALL rows, including the future test rows.
leaky_scaler = StandardScaler()
leaky_scaler.fit(X)
leaky_train = leaky_scaler.transform(X_train)
leaky_test = leaky_scaler.transform(X_test)

# (b) Clean: the scaler sits inside a pipeline and learns from the training rows only.
clean_knn = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5))
clean_line = make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), LinearRegression())

# Plain linear regression: scaling only rescales the weights, so the leak
# changes nothing.
leaky_line = LinearRegression()
leaky_line.fit(leaky_train, price[:n_train])
leaky_predictions = leaky_line.predict(leaky_test)
clean_line.fit(X_train, price[:n_train])
clean_predictions = clean_line.predict(X_test)
print("linear regression predictions identical:", np.allclose(leaky_predictions, clean_predictions))

# Nearest neighbours compare distances, so the scaler's numbers matter.
leaky_knn = KNeighborsClassifier(n_neighbors=5)
leaky_knn.fit(leaky_train, label[:n_train])
leaky_accuracy = leaky_knn.score(leaky_test, label[n_train:])
clean_knn.fit(X_train, label[:n_train])
clean_accuracy = clean_knn.score(X_test, label[n_train:])
print("nearest neighbours, scaler fit on all rows:", leaky_accuracy)
print("nearest neighbours, scaler inside pipeline:", clean_accuracy)

# Output:
# linear regression predictions identical: True
# nearest neighbours, scaler fit on all rows: 0.975
# nearest neighbours, scaler inside pipeline: 0.825
# The leaky scaler already knew where the drifted test rows sit, so the
# nearest-neighbour score looks
# better than it will be in production. The clean, lower number is the
# honest one.`}
    />
    <p>
      Linear regression only rescales its weights to undo any scaling, so its predictions are identical either way.
      A nearest-neighbour model compares distances, and the leaky scaler has already seen where the drifted test
      rows sit, which shrinks the drift. Its score (0.975) is better than the honest one (0.825) that production
      will give. With a penalty on the weights (week 3) the leak would affect linear models too.
    </p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Fitting the scaler or encoder before the train/test split.</strong> Test statistics leak into training
      and the pipeline will not match production. Split first; fit on train; use a Pipeline.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Integer-coding nominal categories for a linear model.</strong> It invents an order. One-hot them, or use
      target encoding or embeddings for many categories.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Scaling inputs to a tree model and believing it helped.</strong> It cannot; splits are invariant. If the
      score changed, something else changed (randomness, a different seed).
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Min-max scaling a column with outliers.</strong> One extreme value flattens everything else. Use
      standardization, robust scaling or a log transform.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Target encoding with each row’s own label.</strong> The feature contains the answer; training looks
      brilliant and the test set does not. Out-of-fold and smoothed, or not at all.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Scaling the target and reporting the metric in scaled units.</strong> An RMSE of 0.3 in z-scores means
      nothing to anyone. Invert the transform and report in dollars, days or whatever the target was.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Forgetting the unseen category.</strong> The first production row with a new city crashes the encoder
      unless you told it what to do.
    </p>
  </Callout>

